# Player-Data/ is per-deployment runtime identity (certificates, keys,
# offline-phase preprocessing material) -- gitignored, but NOT excluded
# from the Docker build context before this file existed, so `COPY . .`
# in the top-level Dockerfile baked in whatever happened to be sitting in
# the repo's own Player-Data/ at build time (here: ~2GB / 1133 files of
# leftover material from unrelated past benchmark sessions -- old certs,
# old Mascot-Secrets/ChaiGear-Secrets preprocessing batches for other
# protocols and N values). Docker auto-seeds a freshly-created named
# volume from whatever the image already has at that mount path, so
# every node container's Player-Data volume -- even brand new -- was
# actually starting pre-populated with this stale cross-session content,
# not empty. Confirmed directly: every node's own P<id>.pem had the
# image build's date, not the container's actual creation time, and a
# node killed mid-computation triggered a reproducible MAC-check
# assertion crash (mac_fail_remove's unlink() failing) on every
# subsequent run until the volume was manually wiped -- consistent with
# stale preprocessing state being read back in, not regenerated fresh.
# node/entrypoint.sh already intentionally keeps a party's cert across
# restarts via a real mounted volume; that identity should come from the
# volume alone, never from the image.
Player-Data
